@lisp
(define (accumulate-n op init seqs)
  (if (null? (car seqs))
      nil
      (cons (accumulate op init ⟨??⟩)
            (accumulate-n op init ⟨??⟩))))
@end lisp
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.37}Exercise 2.37:}
Suppose we represent vectors @b{v} = @math{{(v_i)}} as sequences of numbers, and
matrices @b{m} = @math{{(m_{ij})}} as sequences of vectors (the rows of the
matrix).  For example, the matrix
@displaymath
\left(\matrix{	1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \cr
		4 & 5 & 6 & 6 \cr
		6 & 7 & 8 & 9 \cr }\right)
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
+-         -+
|  1 2 3 4  |
|  4 5 6 6  |
|  6 7 8 9  |
+-         -+
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
is represented as the sequence @code{((1 2 3 4) (4 5 6 6) (6 7 8 9))}.  With
this representation, we can use sequence operations to concisely express the
basic matrix and vector operations.  These operations (which are described in
any book on matrix algebra) are the following:
@displaymath
\eqalign{ 
	\hbox{(dot-product v w)} 	& \hbox{returns the sum}\;\Sigma_i v_i w_i; \cr
	\hbox{(matrix-*-vector m v)} 	& \hbox{returns the vector}\;{\bf t}, \cr
		& \hbox{where}\; t_i = \Sigma_j m_{ij} v_j; \cr
	\hbox{(matrix-*-matrix m n)} 	& \hbox{returns the matrix}\;{\bf p}, \cr
		& \hbox{where}\; p_{ij} = \Sigma_k m_{ik} n_{kj}; \cr
	\hbox{(transpose m)} 		& \hbox{returns the matrix}\;{\bf n}, \cr
		& \hbox{where}\; n_{ij} = m_{ji}. \cr
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
                                       __
(dot-product v w)      returns the sum >_i v_i w_i

(matrix-*-vector m v)  returns the vector t,
                                   __
                       where t_i = >_j m_(ij) v_j

(matrix-*-matrix m n)  returns the matrix p,
                                      __
                       where p_(ij) = >_k m_(ik) n_(kj)

(transpose m)          returns the matrix n,
                       where n_(ij) = m_(ji)
@end example

@end ifinfo
We can define the dot product as@footnote{This definition uses the extended
version of @code{map} described in @ref{Footnote 78}.}

@lisp
(define (dot-product v w)
  (accumulate + 0 (map * v w)))
@end lisp

Fill in the missing expressions in the following procedures for computing the
other matrix operations.  (The procedure @code{accumulate-n} is defined in
@ref{Exercise 2.36}.)

@lisp
(define (matrix-*-vector m v)
  (map ⟨??⟩ m))

(define (transpose mat)
  (accumulate-n ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩ mat))

(define (matrix-*-matrix m n)
  (let ((cols (transpose n)))
    (map ⟨??⟩ m)))
@end lisp
@end quotation

